ormally, during fine-tuning: w'=w_0+\delta w and the output is y=xw'. therefore, y=x(w_0+\delta w) so  oxed{y=xw_0+x\delta w} the problem is that \delta w has exactly the same size as w_0 . for a 4096	imes4096 matrix, 4096	imes4096=16,777,216 parameters must potentially be trained. lora says: rather than learning the entire \delta w , learn a low-rank approximation. the book describes lora as learning smaller matrices a and b whose product approximates the weight update. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) 2. lora forward computation there are two notational conventions in the materials, so this is an important exam point. book convention the raschka implementation uses a\in\mathbb{r}^{d	imes r} and b\in\mathbb{r}^{r	imes d_{	ext{out}}}. thus ab\in\mathbb{r}^{d	imes d_{	ext{out}}}, which is exactly the shape of w_0 . the lora layer computes:  oxed{ y=xw_0+\alpha xab } in the book's implementation. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) equivalently, y=x(w_0+\alpha ab). so the effective weight is  oxed{w_{	ext{eff}}=w_0+\alpha ab} for the book's notation. peft-slide convention the slides use the other ordering: b\in\mathbb{r}^{d	imes r},\qquad a\in\mathbb{r}^{r	imes d_{	ext{out}}}, so their update is written as something like w'=w_0+ rac{\alpha}{r}ba. the mathematics is the same idea; only the names/order and scaling convention differ . do not blindly memorize whether the smaller matrix is called a or b . for an exam, follow the notation given in the question. 3. why initialize one matrix to zero? this is one of the most important ideas in the entire lora topic. the book initializes: a=	ext{random} and  oxed{b=0}. its implementation uses kaiming-uniform initialization for a and zeros for b . (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) therefore initially, ab=a0=0. so w_{	ext{eff}} = w_0+\alpha ab = w_0. hence:  oxed{y_{	ext{initial}}=xw_0} the lora branch contributes nothing initially . the book explicitly notes that this leaves the pretrained model's behavior unchanged at initialization. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) 4. why is this useful? imagine your pretrained model already works reasonably well. you do not want lora to suddenly inject a random perturbation into every projection. if a and b were randomly initialized, then ab
eq0 and therefore w_{	ext{eff}}
eq w_0. you would immediately change the pretrained model's behavior before training. instead:  oxed{b=0ightarrow ab=0ightarrow w_{	ext{eff}}=w_0} so training begins from the original model. 5. a subtle but very important gradient idea now comes a beautiful part of lora. suppose y=xw_0+\alpha xab and let g= rac{\partial l}{\partial y}. the base weight w_0 is frozen. the trainable variables are a and b . for the book's notation: a:d	imes r,\qquad b:r	imes d_{	ext{out}}. the gradients are  oxed{  rac{\partial l}{\partial b} = \alpha (xa)^t g } and  oxed{  rac{\partial l}{\partial a} = \alpha x^t g b^t } these equations are a mathematical derivation from the lora forward equation. 6. what happens on the very first training step? initially: b=0. therefore:  rac{\partial l}{\partial a} = \alpha x^tgb^t = 0. but:  rac{\partial l}{\partial b} = \alpha(xa)^tg can be nonzero because a is randomly initialized. so initially:  oxed{
