eq0 } ↓  oxed{ a	ext{ also starts receiving gradients} } this is an excellent exam/interview question. 7. why not initialize both a and b to zero? because then: a=0,\qquad b=0. the gradients become:  rac{\partial l}{\partial b} = \alpha(xa)^tg = 0 and  rac{\partial l}{\partial a} = \alpha x^tgb^t = 0. so both matrices remain stuck at zero. thus:  oxed{	ext{both-zero initialization is a bad idea}} the useful initialization is asymmetric:  oxed{a	ext{ random},\quad b=0} for the book's convention. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) 8. numerical example — first lora gradient let's make this completely concrete. suppose x=  egin{bmatrix} 1&2 \end{bmatrix} and a=  egin{bmatrix} 0.5\ 1 \end{bmatrix}. let b=  egin{bmatrix} 0 \end{bmatrix} and \alpha=2. suppose the pretrained layer produces xw_0=3 and target is t=5. use l= rac12(y-t)^2. because b=0 , y=3. therefore  rac{\partial l}{\partial y} = y-t = 3-5=-2. so g=-2. first compute xa = [1\quad2]  egin{bmatrix} 0.5\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = 2.5. then  rac{\partial l}{\partial b} = \alpha(xa)^tg. therefore:  rac{\partial l}{\partial b} = 2(2.5)(-2) =  oxed{-10}. and because b=0 ,  rac{\partial l}{\partial a}=0. so:  oxed{ 
