abla_{w_0}l. its gradient is unnecessary for updating the model because it is frozen. 13. why does this save memory? for a normal 4096	imes4096 matrix: n_{	ext{full}}=4096^2 =16,777,216. with rank r=8, lora stores: 4096(8)+(8)(4096) so n_{	ext{lora}} = 65,536. thus:  rac{16,777,216}{65,536} =  oxed{256}. so the trainable parameter count for that matrix is reduced by  oxed{256	imes}. this exact example appears in the peft slides. (source: peft-slides) 14. what exactly does rank control? remember: ab has rank at most r :  oxed{\operatorname{rank}(ab)\le r}. so r controls the maximum complexity/capacity of the learned update. small r fewer parameters: r\downarrow ightarrow 	ext{cheaper} but the update has a smaller low-rank subspace. large r more parameters: r\uparrow ightarrow 	ext{more trainable capacity} but more memory/computation. the book explicitly describes rank as the setting controlling the number of additional trainable parameters. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) 15. what does alpha do? the book defines \alpha as a scaling factor controlling the influence of the lora adaptation on the original layer output. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) in the book notation: y=xw_0+\alpha xab. think of: xab as the direction/content of the adaptation , while \alpha controls how strongly it contributes . for example, if xab=0.5 then: \alpha=1 	ext{lora contribution}=0.5 \alpha=4 	ext{lora contribution}=2 so increasing \alpha strengthens the adapter's effect. correction: use the implementation's actual scale: book alpha multiplying xab differs from conventional alpha/r. they are equivalent only after matching effective scales; alpha values cannot transfer blindly. complete grounded review 16. the peft-slide convention: \alpha/r the peft slides use the more common scaled form:  oxed{ w'=w_0+ rac{\alpha}{r}ba } rather than the book's simplified implementation w'=w_0+\alpha ab. this is why you may encounter:  rac{\alpha}{r} in papers/libraries and simply \alpha in the raschka implementation. example suppose: r=8,\qquad\alpha=16. then:  rac{\alpha}{r} =  rac{16}{8} =  oxed{2}. so the low-rank update is scaled by 2. the slides also note that values such as \alpha equal to, half of, or double the rank are common choices in their example. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) 17. full training flow now we can finally see the complete lora training loop. step 1 — start with pretrained model w_0 contains all the knowledge acquired during pretraining. step 2 — freeze w_0  oxed{w_0	ext{ does not change}} step 3 — insert lora matrices a,\ b are added to selected linear layers. step 4 — initialize a=	ext{random} b=0. therefore: w_{	ext{eff}}=w_0. step 5 — forward pass y=xw_0+\alpha xab. step 6 — compute loss for a classification task, for example: l=-\log p(y_{	ext{correct}}). step 7 — backpropagation compute: 
abla_a l, \qquad 
abla_b l. step 8 — update only lora parameters a\leftarrow a-\eta
abla_a l b\leftarrow b-\eta
abla_b l. step 9 — repeat after many batches: a,b encode the task-specific adaptation. the book uses its ordinary pytorch training function with an optimizer on the modified model. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) 18. what happened in the book's actual experiment? the book uses a gpt-2-small-sized model with: 124	ext{m} parameters. it freezes the complete model and then adds lora. with r=16,\qquad \alpha=16, the reported trainable lora parameters are:  oxed{2,666,528}. so instead of training roughly 124 million parameters, it trains about 2.7 million. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) the book reports that the initial accuracy was unchanged after adding lora because b=0 , so the lora branch initially produced no modification. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) after training, the example achieved very high performance, with the book reporting approximately: 100\%	ext{ training accuracy} and 96.64\%	ext{ validation accuracy}. the book notes that this involved only around 2.7 million lora parameters rather than the original 124 million model parameters. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) 19. why can lora still train if most parameters are frozen? this is the key conceptual insight. the base model already provides: w_0 which defines a powerful pretrained function. we don't need to completely relearn that function. we only need to learn a relatively small modification: \delta w\approx ab. thus:  oxed{ 	ext{pretrained knowledge} + 	ext{small task-specific correction} } becomes the adaptation strategy. 20. separate lora during deployment after training, we have: w_0,\ a,\ b. we can keep them separate: y=xw_0+\alpha xab. the book emphasizes that keeping lora parameters separate lets the pretrained weights remain unchanged and allows different lora adapters to be associated with the same base model, reducing storage requirements when customizing a model for different applications. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) conceptually: ┌─────────────┐ x ─────────────────►│ base w0 │────┐ └─────────────┘ │ ├──► y x ─► a ─► b ─► scale(α) ───────────────┘ 21. merge lora into the base model eventually we can combine the matrices. for the book notation:  oxed{ w_{	ext{merged}} = w_0+\alpha ab } then inference becomes simply:  oxed{ y=xw_{	ext{merged}} } instead of computing two branches. why is this mathematically identical? start with: y=xw_0+\alpha xab. factor out x : y=x(w_0+\alpha ab). define: w_{	ext{merged}}=w_0+\alpha ab. therefore:  oxed{y=xw_{	ext{merged}}}. nothing about the function changes mathematically. 22. numerical merge example take w_0=  egin{bmatrix} 1&0\ 0&1 \end{bmatrix} and a=  egin{bmatrix} 1\ 2 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad b=  egin{bmatrix} 0.2&0.1 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad \alpha=2. first: ab=  egin{bmatrix} 1\2 \end{bmatrix}  egin{bmatrix} 0.2&0.1 \end{bmatrix} so ab=  egin{bmatrix} 0.2&0.1\ 0.4&0.2 \end{bmatrix}. scale: \alpha ab =  egin{bmatrix} 0.4&0.2\ 0.8&0.4 \end{bmatrix}. merge: w_{	ext{merged}} =  egin{bmatrix} 1&0\ 0&1 \end{bmatrix} +  egin{bmatrix} 0.4&0.2\ 0.8&0.4 \end{bmatrix} giving  oxed{ w_{	ext{merged}} =  egin{bmatrix} 1.4&0.2\ 0.8&1.4 \end{bmatrix} } now take x=[1\quad2]. separate computation base: xw_0=[1\quad2]. lora: xa=1(1)+2(2)=5. therefore: \alpha(xa)b = 2(5)  egin{bmatrix} 0.2&0.1 \end{bmatrix} = [2\quad1]. total: y=[1\quad2]+[2\quad1] =  oxed{[3\quad3]}. merged computation xw_{	ext{merged}} = [1\quad2]  egin{bmatrix} 1.4&0.2\ 0.8&1.4 \end{bmatrix} so = [1.4+1.6,\ 0.2+2.8] =  oxed{[3\quad3]}. exactly the same. 23. why merge? during training, keeping them separate is useful because: a,b are the only trainable components. after training, merging gives: w_{	ext{merged}} so inference can use the ordinary linear layer. this is conceptually: during training  oxed{ w_0 + 	ext{lora branch} } after merge  oxed{ w_{	ext{merged}} } same function, different representation. the book's emphasis is that keeping adapters separate is particularly useful for customization and storage; merging is the natural algebraic equivalent when a standalone adapted model is desired. (source: build a large language model (from scratch)) 24. very important distinction: trainable parameters vs total model parameters suppose: w_0 = 124	ext{m parameters} and lora introduces: 2.7	ext{m}. it does not mean the model suddenly becomes a 2.7m-parameter model. it is still a 124m-parameter base model plus the adapter. the important reduction is:  oxed{ 	ext{trainable parameters} \downarrow } not necessarily:  oxed{ 	ext{total model parameters} \downarrow } this distinction leads directly to the motivation for quantization and qlora , which we will encounter next. 25. exam numerical bank — step 3.4 numerical 1 — parameter count a square matrix has d=4096,\quad r=8. calculate lora trainable parameters. n=r(d+d) =8(4096+4096) =8(8192)  oxed{65,536} numerical 2 — reduction factor full matrix: 4096^2=16,777,216. lora: 65,536. therefore:  rac{16,777,216}{65,536} =  oxed{256	imes}. numerical 3 — rank constraint if a\in\mathbb{r}^{4096	imes8} and b\in\mathbb{r}^{8	imes4096}, then: ab\in\mathbb{r}^{4096	imes4096} and  oxed{\operatorname{rank}(ab)\le8}. numerical 4 — first-step gradient trap given b=0, what is  rac{\partial l}{\partial a} = \alpha x^tgb^t? answer:  oxed{0}. but \partial l/\partial b can be nonzero because a
