 sampling vs ordinary sampling ordinary synthetic-data generation q ightarrow 	ext{one teacher response} ightarrow 	ext{dataset}. rejection-sampled generation q ightarrow r_1,\ldots,r_k ightarrow 	ext{verify} ightarrow 	ext{filter} ightarrow 	ext{dataset}. the second pipeline spends more compute up front . but the resulting student data can be much higher quality. therefore:  oxed{ 	ext{more teacher-generation cost} ightarrow 	ext{better training signal} ightarrow 	ext{better student} } this is why the source describes the distillation dataset generation as a deliberate quality-control process. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 5.16.36 the economics then make sense the teacher-generation stage is expensive. but once the data exists:  oxed{ 	ext{one teacher} ightarrow 	ext{one reusable dataset} } and then:  oxed{ 	ext{many students} ightarrow 	ext{same dataset} }. hence the earlier point:  oxed{ 	ext{teacher cost is amortized} }. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 5.16.37 dataset design determines specialization let's make this concrete. suppose a hypothetical dataset were: 95\%	ext{ coding}, \quad 5\%	ext{ general}. then a student might become highly code-oriented. conversely: 95\%	ext{ general conversation}, \quad 5\%	ext{ reasoning}. then the reasoning behavior would receive much less supervision. deepseek instead uses:  oxed{ 	ext{heavy reasoning data} + 	ext{meaningful general data} } to produce a model that is both reasoning-capable and still useful as an assistant. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 5.16.38 one subtle point: the 800k examples are not all the same kind of supervision there are at least two broad categories:  oxed{ 	ext{reasoning examples} } and:  oxed{ 	ext{general-purpose examples} }. reasoning examples emphasize: 	ext{long-form problem solving}. general examples emphasize: 	ext{normal assistant behavior}. so the student objective is effectively aggregating multiple behavioral distributions into one model. 5.16.39 this connects directly to catastrophic forgetting we have already learned that fine-tuning can shift a pretrained model toward the training distribution. here, imagine: 	ext{base model} already knows: 	ext{conversation + qa + translation + coding + math}. if distillation data contained only math reasoning, sft could disproportionately reinforce math and potentially weaken the balance of other behaviors. the source doesn't present a formal catastrophic-forgetting experiment here, so we should not claim a measured effect from this chapter. but it explicitly states that the general 178k samples are intended to prevent the distilled models from becoming narrow specialists. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) 5.16.40 a compact mathematical model of the dataset we can conceptualize the student data distribution as:  oxed{ p_{	ext{student-data}} = \sum_d \lambda_d p_d } where each d is a domain: d\in \{	ext{math, code, stem, logic, general}\}. and: \sum_d\lambda_d=1. approximately: \lambda_{	ext{math}}\approx0.49 \lambda_{	ext{code}}\approx0.26 \lambda_{	ext{general}}\approx0.22 \lambda_{	ext{stem}}\approx0.012 \lambda_{	ext{logic}}\approx0.012. these are derived from the source's rounded counts. the student's loss is therefore effectively averaging supervision across these domain-specific distributions. 5.16.41 a useful training objective interpretation if: l_d(	heta) is the sft loss on domain d , then conceptually:  oxed{ l(	heta) = \sum_d \lambda_dl_d(	heta) } this is not stated as deepseek's explicit published loss equation for the dataset mixture; it is the natural mathematical interpretation of training on a mixed dataset. it explains why dataset composition matters. increasing: \lambda_{	ext{math}} increases the relative training pressure from mathematical examples. 5.16.42 why this is more than “just 800k examples” the important object is not merely: n=800,000. it is:  oxed{ (n,	ext{quality},	ext{distribution},	ext{length},	ext{verification}) } all together. two datasets could both contain 800k examples but yield very different students. for example: d_1= 	ext{high-quality verified reasoning}. versus: d_2= 	ext{uncurated teacher outputs}. same sample count. very different training signal. 5.16.43 the key design principle the source's approach can be summarized as:  oxed{ 	ext{generate broadly} ightarrow 	ext{verify strictly} ightarrow 	ext{filter stylistically} ightarrow 	ext{mix domains deliberately} ightarrow 	ext{sft student} } that is the actual engineering recipe behind the famous “800k distillation” result. 5.16.44 exam numerical set numerical 1 — domain percentage math: 395k total: 804k.  rac{395}{804}	imes100 \approx  oxed{49.1\%}. numerical 2 — reasoning fraction math + code: 395+211=606k.  rac{606}{804}	imes100 \approx  oxed{75.4\%}. numerical 3 — general fraction  rac{178}{804}	imes100 \approx  oxed{22.1\%}. numerical 4 — total tokens 804,745	imes5,355 \approx  oxed{4.31	ext{ billion tokens}}. numerical 5 — multiple teacher attempts suppose one response has correctness probability: p=0.4. five independent responses: p(\ge1	ext{ correct}) = 1-(1-p)^5. =1-0.6^5  oxed{\approx92.2\%}. numerical 6 — dataset mixture suppose a batch has 64 examples following the approximate domain proportions. expected math examples: 64(0.491) \approx  oxed{31.4}. expected code: 64(0.262) \approx  oxed{16.8}. expected general: 64(0.221) \approx  oxed{14.1}. the actual batch will of course contain integer counts; these are expected values under random sampling from the approximate mixture. 5.16.45 exam-style conceptual questions q1. why is rejection sampling necessary? because a teacher can generate incorrect or poorly formatted responses. keeping only high-quality verified responses creates a stronger supervised signal for the student. (source: build a deepseek model (from scratch)) q2. why is code easier to verify than general conversation? code can often be executed and tested, while open-ended gener